WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.720
This tiny robot may look unassuming,

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.160
but even at a mere 100 grams—

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.400
about as heavy as a bar of soap—

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.320
the “FlyCroTug” can pull up to 40 times its own weight..

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.900
To create the mighty FlyCroTug -- named for its

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.340
flying, micro, tugging features --

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.590
researchers took a cue from wasps.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.960
Typically, these insects use their stinger to subdue prey

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.420
before transporting it back to their nest.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.640
If the prey is too heavy to fly with,

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.000
some wasps plant their feet on the ground

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.820
and pull their prey home.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.720
Similarly, when a payload is too heavy for flight --

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.340
anything bigger than the robot itself --

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.900
the FlyCroTug stays on terra firma,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.620
whereit uses adhesives and tiny metal hooks called

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.440
microspines to stick to a surface,

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.600
and a powerful tether to tug on an object.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.580
Researchers demonstrated the FlyCroTug’s capabilities

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.420
by anchoring it atop a partially collapsed building

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.040
and having it haul up a bulky set of sensors

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.800
to inspect small openings in the rubble.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.280
FlyCroTugs can also work together to open doors

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.860
one pulls the handle downwards

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.760
while sticking to the door itself,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.220
and another pulls the door open

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.540
while anchored to the ground.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.720
The FlyCroTug still has room for improvement -

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.900
it’s 5 minute flight time limits the robots

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.960
to only short-range operations

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.960
and more difficult, multi-step tasks

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.660
require more than one FlyCroTug.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.440
Still, FlyCroTug’s unprecedented strength

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.500
gives it an advantage over other miniature drones

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.600
which—due to their size—can typically

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.200
only survey their environment

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.320
instead of actually interacting with it.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.180
That could make this new class of robots

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.640
useful in everything from

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.820
planting sensors in hard-to-reach spots

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.380
to removing debris in disaster zones.

